    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Root position on this set isn't new material, either. Read the bottom three strings of the open C chord — "
        },
        { "text": "x 3 2 0 1 0", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", strings 5-4-3 — and it's exactly this grip. " },
        {
          "text": "You already met this reading",
          "link": { "kind": "article", "slug": "triad-open-chords-you-know" }
        },
        {
          "text": "; here it's not a surprise, it's the anchor everything else on this page hangs off."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Two things are true about why this set sounds full, and they're not the same kind of true. Strings 5, 4, and 3 open up as "
        },
        { "text": "A2 D3 G3", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " — 110, 147, and 196 Hz — sitting right on an acoustic guitar's own two main body resonances: the air inside the box rings near 100 Hz, and the top itself near 200 Hz. The instrument reinforces this register; that part is measurable."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The rest of \"full\" is convention, and it's worth saying so plainly: most comping vocabulary was built in this register, which is a large part of why it sounds like home. That's not the same claim as \"the ear is most sensitive here\" — it isn't. Sensitivity to loudness actually peaks much higher, around 2 to 4 kHz."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Two sets down, two different single notes have moved — the middle one, then the top one. Nothing on this set is bent out of shape at all: strings 3-4-5 are the plain version, and it's the sets above it that turn out to be the adjusted ones. There's a reason exactly one string pair causes that, and "
        },
        {
          "text": "it's coming",
          "link": { "kind": "article", "slug": "triad-the-b-string-shift" }
        },
        { "text": "." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Next: "
        },
        {
          "text": "one set lower still",
          "link": { "kind": "article", "slug": "triad-major-strings-4-5-6" }
        },
        {
          "text": ", where the shape stops changing entirely and the real question becomes whether to use it at all."
        }
      ]
    }
